1
00:01:51,111 --> 00:01:55,911
Listen sir, we take our jobs very seriously. It's not wise to mark our shipwrights like that.

2
00:01:58,911 --> 00:02:00,611
Oops. Sorry.

3
00:02:08,811 --> 00:02:13,611
You're on shipwright journey. This is what happens when you try to pull that pirate crap around here.

4
00:02:15,111 --> 00:02:17,111
It's that tower right ahead of us.

5
00:02:21,111 --> 00:02:27,611
Everything's huge! So cool!

6
00:02:29,611 --> 00:02:36,611
They're strong, they're skilled, and they're the pride of every citizen in Water 7.

7
00:02:42,611 --> 00:02:44,611
It's scary!

8
00:02:46,611 --> 00:02:48,611
What is that guy selling? It looks creepy.

9
00:02:48,611 --> 00:02:50,611
It's just a mask shop.

10
00:02:51,111 --> 00:02:51,611
It's scary.

11
00:02:58,611 --> 00:03:00,611
CP-9

12
00:03:06,611 --> 00:03:07,611
Robin?

13
00:03:07,611 --> 00:03:17,111
Pity, I was hoping that we could kill you in your sleep.

14
00:03:17,111 --> 00:03:18,611
The Frankie Family and Iceberg!

15
00:03:22,611 --> 00:03:26,611
You got a lot of nerve. Who are you?

16
00:03:26,611 --> 00:03:27,111
You got a lot of nerve. Who are you?

17
00:03:27,111 --> 00:03:39,111
You got a lot of nerve. Who are you?

18
00:03:39,111 --> 00:03:53,611
You don't know who we are?

19
00:03:53,611 --> 00:03:54,611
Really?

20
00:03:54,611 --> 00:03:58,111
Because we sure know you, pirate hunter.

21
00:03:58,111 --> 00:04:03,111
Babies stop crying when they hear our name.

22
00:04:03,111 --> 00:04:05,111
A collection of bounty hunters!

23
00:04:05,111 --> 00:04:08,111
We are the Frankie Family!

24
00:04:08,111 --> 00:04:13,611
Now if you don't mind, I think we'll be taking that 60 million bounty you've got on your head.

25
00:04:13,611 --> 00:04:19,611
When we're done with you, we'll wait inside that cabin of yours and ambush the rest of your crew when they get back.

26
00:04:21,611 --> 00:04:23,611
We're going to be rolling in the dough in no time!

27
00:04:23,611 --> 00:04:27,111
It's my lucky day!

28
00:04:31,111 --> 00:04:32,111
Lucky, huh?

29
00:04:35,111 --> 00:04:37,111
We'll see about that now, won't we?

30
00:04:37,111 --> 00:04:45,611
And be all mine!

31
00:04:46,111 --> 00:04:47,611
Two swords, entendeu?

32
00:04:47,611 --> 00:04:48,611
Two swords, Tile!

33
00:04:48,611 --> 00:04:49,611
Rhino!

34
00:04:50,111 --> 00:04:51,611
Psychos!

35
00:04:56,611 --> 00:04:57,611
Yeah.

36
00:05:02,611 --> 00:05:03,611
Back to sleep.

37
00:05:03,611 --> 00:05:05,611
Oh

38
00:05:05,611 --> 00:05:08,251
Oh, man!

39
00:05:19,191 --> 00:05:20,351
This is the place.

40
00:05:28,531 --> 00:05:30,291
Hey, whatever you want, make it quick.

41
00:05:30,391 --> 00:05:31,551
We're kind of busy right now.

42
00:05:31,811 --> 00:05:33,351
We need to cash in our stuff.

43
00:05:36,431 --> 00:05:37,931
How much do you think it's worth?

44
00:05:40,671 --> 00:05:41,931
Boss, come here, quick!

45
00:05:43,631 --> 00:05:45,011
Gold, this much?

46
00:05:45,211 --> 00:05:46,231
No, this much?

47
00:05:46,411 --> 00:05:48,511
Then why are you wasting your time talking to me?

48
00:05:48,671 --> 00:05:50,611
Escort them to the VIP room right now.

49
00:05:50,771 --> 00:05:52,131
We can't afford to lose this deal.

50
00:05:52,271 --> 00:05:52,731
Got it?

51
00:05:55,651 --> 00:05:56,771
Wow, comfy!

52
00:05:57,471 --> 00:05:58,811
After we cash in our gold,

53
00:05:58,871 --> 00:06:00,311
we got to get one of these for the merry.

54
00:06:00,491 --> 00:06:02,151
I can bounce on this all day.

55
00:06:05,611 --> 00:06:12,191
Hey, you're not saying anything.

56
00:06:12,731 --> 00:06:14,531
Don't tell me you think this stuff is fake.

57
00:06:14,711 --> 00:06:15,891
No, far from it.

58
00:06:16,051 --> 00:06:18,471
I've been an appraiser for 25 years, so I know.

59
00:06:18,471 --> 00:06:23,471
Um, this treasure is valued at...

60
00:06:24,291 --> 00:06:27,011
100 million berries?

61
00:06:27,391 --> 00:06:28,331
Sit down, stupid!

62
00:06:28,671 --> 00:06:31,371
You want the whole city to know how much cash we're lugging around?

63
00:06:31,491 --> 00:06:33,791
You're really going to give us that much money, mister?

64
00:06:33,791 --> 00:06:34,851
Play it cool, Loopy.

65
00:06:35,131 --> 00:06:36,951
You've got to handle these things like a professional!

66
00:06:37,591 --> 00:06:38,791
It may sound like a lot,

67
00:06:38,871 --> 00:06:40,351
but that's most certainly what it's worth.

68
00:06:40,471 --> 00:06:42,611
It has historical value and high purity.

69
00:06:42,611 --> 00:06:45,271
With 100 million,

70
00:06:45,391 --> 00:06:47,331
we can make the merry even better than before!

71
00:06:47,611 --> 00:06:49,411
No kidding, and we'll have plenty to spare!

72
00:06:49,711 --> 00:06:52,551
Then we'll spend the rest on a big statue to put on the deck!

73
00:06:52,611 --> 00:06:53,831
No, no, we won't.

74
00:06:53,931 --> 00:06:54,691
Well, that's wonderful.

75
00:06:54,911 --> 00:06:56,151
I'm glad you accept the amount.

76
00:06:56,331 --> 00:06:58,311
Now I need your thumbprint and signature here.

77
00:06:58,731 --> 00:07:01,371
After that, I'll prepare your money lickety-split.

78
00:07:01,771 --> 00:07:03,851
You can have both my thumbs if you want them!

79
00:07:03,911 --> 00:07:04,491
Mine too!

80
00:07:04,671 --> 00:07:06,431
The more thumbs, the better, right?

81
00:07:06,431 --> 00:07:06,931
What?

82
00:07:17,051 --> 00:07:18,191
Uh, what's up?

83
00:07:18,351 --> 00:07:19,231
Giving him a tillprint?

84
00:07:19,511 --> 00:07:20,731
Before we sign anything,

85
00:07:20,891 --> 00:07:22,551
there are three things I'd like to say.

86
00:07:23,311 --> 00:07:24,831
Mr. Appraiser.

87
00:07:25,831 --> 00:07:28,391
First, I forgot to mention this,

88
00:07:28,671 --> 00:07:31,151
but he's got a 100 million bounty on his head.

89
00:07:33,491 --> 00:07:34,171
Second...

90
00:07:34,171 --> 00:07:37,231
I don't accept the appraisal you gave us.

91
00:07:37,611 --> 00:07:37,931
Third,

92
00:07:38,551 --> 00:07:40,431
if you try to lie to us again,

93
00:07:41,131 --> 00:07:43,111
it'll be the last lie you ever tell.

94
00:07:43,651 --> 00:07:44,291
You got that?

95
00:07:44,611 --> 00:07:44,991
Ah!

96
00:07:45,991 --> 00:07:47,631
Please forgive me!

97
00:07:48,931 --> 00:07:50,631
Three hundred million?

98
00:07:51,431 --> 00:07:53,311
This is like a dream come true!

99
00:07:54,051 --> 00:07:55,831
Skypeia paid off after all!

100
00:07:55,891 --> 00:07:57,431
I never thought I'd see the day!

101
00:07:57,631 --> 00:07:59,791
You know, I always wanted to be filthy rugs!

102
00:07:59,791 --> 00:08:00,811
It's our money too!

103
00:08:01,151 --> 00:08:02,791
Pleasure doing business with you!

104
00:08:02,791 --> 00:08:05,551
I'm so comfortable having all this money in one place, guys!

105
00:08:05,871 --> 00:08:06,631
It's scary!

106
00:08:07,051 --> 00:08:10,051
I mean, what if I trip and it all falls out or something?

107
00:08:11,051 --> 00:08:12,051
That was great!

108
00:08:12,171 --> 00:08:14,171
You sure were scary back there, Nami?

109
00:08:14,431 --> 00:08:16,971
I estimated 300 million from the start,

110
00:08:17,051 --> 00:08:18,711
but I figured he wouldn't give it up easy.

111
00:08:19,091 --> 00:08:20,931
You really got to work on your game face.

112
00:08:21,051 --> 00:08:21,971
Good thing I was there.

113
00:08:22,091 --> 00:08:22,811
Cut it out?

114
00:08:23,091 --> 00:08:24,911
You're making me nervous swinging that around!

115
00:08:24,911 --> 00:08:26,811
Well, whatever.

116
00:08:26,951 --> 00:08:28,951
We got our money now, and that's what matters.

117
00:08:31,091 --> 00:08:33,291
You dropped it!

118
00:08:33,871 --> 00:08:35,051
Don't let it fall in the room!

119
00:08:39,671 --> 00:08:42,131
I would have been bad if the money got wet, huh?

120
00:08:42,131 --> 00:08:46,271
If you lose my money, you're paying it!

121
00:08:46,271 --> 00:08:47,851
Keep going to crumble down your throat!

122
00:08:48,431 --> 00:08:49,391
I'm sorry.

123
00:08:49,771 --> 00:08:50,691
Very sorry.

124
00:08:50,951 --> 00:08:53,471
But please, don't break my face anymore.

125
00:08:55,551 --> 00:08:55,991
Whoa.

126
00:08:56,251 --> 00:08:57,111
Did you hear that?

127
00:08:57,791 --> 00:08:58,471
Sure did.

128
00:08:58,591 --> 00:09:00,311
That's 300 million berries down there.

129
00:09:00,391 --> 00:09:01,431
Ain't no doubt about it.

130
00:09:01,471 --> 00:09:02,611
Those are the straw hats.

131
00:09:02,651 --> 00:09:04,591
I thought grabbing their bounty was a sweet deal,

132
00:09:04,691 --> 00:09:05,791
but this is way sweeter.

133
00:09:06,171 --> 00:09:07,751
If we meet up with the rest of the gang

134
00:09:07,751 --> 00:09:08,851
and get them to help us out,

135
00:09:08,971 --> 00:09:10,131
we can make twice the money

136
00:09:10,131 --> 00:09:11,551
with only half the hard work.

137
00:09:14,831 --> 00:09:18,331
I don't know, Nami.

138
00:09:18,711 --> 00:09:20,051
Maybe we should take the cash back

139
00:09:20,051 --> 00:09:21,151
to the ship after all.

140
00:09:21,471 --> 00:09:23,231
That'd be a complete waste of time.

141
00:09:23,351 --> 00:09:24,811
The shipyard is right around the corner.

142
00:09:25,431 --> 00:09:26,091
Come on.

143
00:09:26,191 --> 00:09:27,831
You don't have to ride so far away.

144
00:09:27,991 --> 00:09:29,271
Let me play with the money.

145
00:09:29,431 --> 00:09:30,991
Anyway, we'll probably head back

146
00:09:30,991 --> 00:09:32,231
to the Mary soon enough.

147
00:09:32,391 --> 00:09:33,771
After we meet with the shipwrights,

148
00:09:33,771 --> 00:09:35,351
I'm sure they'll need to look at the damages

149
00:09:35,351 --> 00:09:36,751
before they can give us a quote.

150
00:09:36,751 --> 00:09:38,251
I didn't think of it that way.

151
00:09:38,571 --> 00:09:40,191
I guess I could watch the money till then.

152
00:09:40,471 --> 00:09:42,771
I've never carried so much cash at once, that's all.

153
00:09:42,911 --> 00:09:44,151
I don't know how to handle it.

154
00:09:44,351 --> 00:09:45,611
Well, that's only natural.

155
00:09:46,051 --> 00:09:47,211
Just don't flail it around

156
00:09:47,211 --> 00:09:48,311
and throw it in any rivers

157
00:09:48,311 --> 00:09:49,211
and you'll be fine.

158
00:09:49,371 --> 00:09:51,431
Ooh, they're selling water buns over there.

159
00:09:51,531 --> 00:09:52,651
Let's buy a hundred of them.

160
00:09:52,771 --> 00:09:53,231
Shut up.

161
00:09:53,551 --> 00:09:54,391
How about a thousand?

162
00:09:54,571 --> 00:09:55,091
Shut up.

163
00:09:55,111 --> 00:09:56,051
How about shut up?

164
00:09:56,851 --> 00:09:57,251
Okay.

165
00:10:06,751 --> 00:10:27,351
Right, back to the shipyard.

166
00:10:27,691 --> 00:10:29,711
And it looks like the crowd's cleared out too.

167
00:10:29,711 --> 00:10:32,591
Well, that's good.

168
00:10:32,931 --> 00:10:33,911
We have enough to deal with

169
00:10:33,911 --> 00:10:36,051
without the shipwright fan club getting in the way.

170
00:10:36,551 --> 00:10:38,491
All we gotta do is find that ice guy.

171
00:10:38,911 --> 00:10:41,451
Uh, wait, what's his name again?

172
00:10:41,891 --> 00:10:42,831
It's Iceberg.

173
00:10:43,311 --> 00:10:45,311
Oh yeah, now I remember Ice Pops.

174
00:10:45,431 --> 00:10:46,971
The old lady back at the train station

175
00:10:46,971 --> 00:10:48,451
said that's who we needed to talk to.

176
00:10:48,551 --> 00:10:49,651
But now that I think about it,

177
00:10:49,691 --> 00:10:51,331
we don't know anything about him, do we?

178
00:10:51,371 --> 00:10:52,671
Well, it's the only lead we've got.

179
00:10:52,831 --> 00:10:54,391
I don't even know how this place works.

180
00:10:54,571 --> 00:10:55,951
Is it okay just to walk in?

181
00:10:56,651 --> 00:10:57,931
That's what I'm doing.

182
00:10:58,251 --> 00:10:59,191
Not again.

183
00:11:00,691 --> 00:11:02,251
Hold it right there.

184
00:11:02,991 --> 00:11:05,931
You got a lot of gumption just waltzing in like that.

185
00:11:07,271 --> 00:11:09,031
Why don't we take a step over here,

186
00:11:09,151 --> 00:11:10,171
then we can talk.

187
00:11:11,511 --> 00:11:13,171
Don't worry, it's not personal.

188
00:11:13,411 --> 00:11:14,551
You're outsiders is all.

189
00:11:14,791 --> 00:11:16,311
So you're not allowed inside the plant.

190
00:11:18,971 --> 00:11:19,891
Oopsy-daisy.

191
00:11:24,031 --> 00:11:25,031
All right, champ.

192
00:11:25,031 --> 00:11:26,571
What brings you to the docks today?

193
00:11:26,791 --> 00:11:27,611
Come on, Oosop.

194
00:11:27,671 --> 00:11:28,531
I know it's you.

195
00:11:28,711 --> 00:11:30,131
I'm right here, Luffy.

196
00:11:30,831 --> 00:11:31,271
Idiot.

197
00:11:31,531 --> 00:11:32,711
Oh, now that I look at it,

198
00:11:32,751 --> 00:11:33,631
his nose is square.

199
00:11:33,871 --> 00:11:35,411
That means he's square, Oosop.

200
00:11:35,791 --> 00:11:36,191
Awesome!

201
00:11:36,771 --> 00:11:37,931
I'm the only Oosop.

202
00:11:38,071 --> 00:11:38,571
Stop it.

203
00:11:38,991 --> 00:11:40,311
Anyway, that aside,

204
00:11:40,631 --> 00:11:42,111
we came here to see Iceberg,

205
00:11:42,171 --> 00:11:42,811
if that's all right.

206
00:11:42,951 --> 00:11:43,251
Hmm.

207
00:11:43,651 --> 00:11:44,871
Is that so, little miss?

208
00:11:45,111 --> 00:11:45,891
Are you customers?

209
00:11:46,711 --> 00:11:47,691
Oh, that's nifty.

210
00:11:48,231 --> 00:11:49,871
Not every day that youngsters come here

211
00:11:49,871 --> 00:11:50,871
with a letter of introduction

212
00:11:50,871 --> 00:11:51,911
from Granny Kokoro.

213
00:11:52,371 --> 00:11:53,511
Uh, hold on.

214
00:11:53,551 --> 00:11:54,391
How old are you?

215
00:11:54,851 --> 00:11:55,651
I'm 23.

216
00:11:55,811 --> 00:11:56,451
Why do you ask?

217
00:11:56,551 --> 00:11:57,331
You know, for someone

218
00:11:57,331 --> 00:11:58,831
who's hardly any older than us,

219
00:11:58,891 --> 00:12:00,391
you sure do talk like an old man.

220
00:12:01,491 --> 00:12:02,711
I get that a lot.

221
00:12:03,091 --> 00:12:04,311
So do you know Iceberg,

222
00:12:04,391 --> 00:12:05,751
or do we come to the wrong place?

223
00:12:06,031 --> 00:12:07,531
Well, of course I know Iceberg.

224
00:12:07,771 --> 00:12:09,591
Why, I'd say that everyone here knows him,

225
00:12:09,691 --> 00:12:11,611
seeing as he's the mayor of the city and all.

226
00:12:12,471 --> 00:12:12,831
Wow.

227
00:12:13,831 --> 00:12:15,771
Ice Pops is an important guy, huh?

228
00:12:16,051 --> 00:12:17,351
Darn Skippy he is.

229
00:12:17,471 --> 00:12:19,771
He's also the president of our galley law company

230
00:12:19,771 --> 00:12:22,071
and manager of the sea train on top of that.

231
00:12:22,151 --> 00:12:23,711
I didn't know he was such a big deal.

232
00:12:23,931 --> 00:12:25,831
I had a feeling you weren't from around here.

233
00:12:26,171 --> 00:12:28,031
Iceberg is pretty famous in these parts.

234
00:12:28,151 --> 00:12:29,611
But do you know where we can find him?

235
00:12:29,791 --> 00:12:31,731
Hmm, that's the question, isn't it?

236
00:12:32,811 --> 00:12:34,131
He keeps pretty busy, of course.

237
00:12:34,611 --> 00:12:36,631
Hunting him down can be a chore sometimes.

238
00:12:37,071 --> 00:12:38,611
But you work for him, right?

239
00:12:38,771 --> 00:12:40,071
Can't you get in touch somehow?

240
00:12:40,371 --> 00:12:41,311
I suppose I could,

241
00:12:41,411 --> 00:12:43,431
but it seems like you're in a hurry to get this done.

242
00:12:43,431 --> 00:12:44,891
Not exactly, but...

243
00:12:44,891 --> 00:12:47,851
It's fine, I understand.

244
00:12:48,591 --> 00:12:50,631
You're itching to have your ship looked at, right?

245
00:12:51,711 --> 00:12:53,171
Where did you anchor it?

246
00:12:53,711 --> 00:12:55,371
Uh, it's over by the Rocky Cape.

247
00:12:55,771 --> 00:12:56,511
Alrighty then.

248
00:12:56,691 --> 00:12:58,271
I'll skedaddle over there real quick.

249
00:12:58,491 --> 00:13:00,471
If I take a gander at your ship beforehand,

250
00:13:00,651 --> 00:13:02,731
you can start talking cost with Iceberg right away.

251
00:13:03,091 --> 00:13:04,831
As I said, he's very busy,

252
00:13:04,991 --> 00:13:06,671
so I'm sure he'll be grateful as well.

253
00:13:06,871 --> 00:13:08,471
Wait, skedaddle like how?

254
00:13:08,891 --> 00:13:10,211
On a yagata or what?

255
00:13:10,211 --> 00:13:13,531
I thought we were trying to save time.

256
00:13:14,091 --> 00:13:16,091
If you folks can hang tight for ten minutes,

257
00:13:16,211 --> 00:13:18,151
I should be back in two shakes of a lamb's tail.

258
00:13:18,511 --> 00:13:19,431
Just ten minutes?

259
00:13:20,431 --> 00:13:21,171
That's right.

260
00:13:21,591 --> 00:13:22,731
For your money back.

261
00:13:25,671 --> 00:13:26,591
Whoa!

262
00:13:29,251 --> 00:13:30,691
He's fast!

263
00:13:32,351 --> 00:13:33,111
Whoa!

264
00:13:35,111 --> 00:13:35,571
Whoa!

265
00:13:36,271 --> 00:13:37,011
He flew!

266
00:13:37,011 --> 00:13:41,171
Wait, where's he going?

267
00:13:41,631 --> 00:13:43,651
If he jumps off the edge, he'll fall!

268
00:13:44,651 --> 00:13:45,191
Whoa!

269
00:13:45,351 --> 00:13:46,151
Come back!

270
00:13:55,431 --> 00:13:56,851
So cool!

271
00:13:57,311 --> 00:13:58,691
Yeah, but is he gonna be okay?

272
00:13:59,691 --> 00:14:00,551
Don't worry.

273
00:14:01,091 --> 00:14:02,891
He's always soaring through town.

274
00:14:02,891 --> 00:14:03,491
Huh?

275
00:14:04,571 --> 00:14:07,091
They call him the Mountain Wind.

276
00:14:07,891 --> 00:14:09,151
If you didn't know,

277
00:14:09,451 --> 00:14:12,611
he's the Galila Company's Doc One carpentry foreman.

278
00:14:14,611 --> 00:14:15,091
Kaku.

279
00:14:19,491 --> 00:14:20,511
I see.

280
00:14:20,711 --> 00:14:22,531
So this is the City of Water, huh?

281
00:14:23,551 --> 00:14:25,111
Pretty impressive place.

282
00:14:25,271 --> 00:14:26,751
Lives up to its name, that's for sure.

283
00:14:26,751 --> 00:14:28,471
I mean, look around.

284
00:14:28,671 --> 00:14:30,891
Clean water, clean streets, fresh food,

285
00:14:30,971 --> 00:14:33,191
and most importantly, plenty of cute girls.

286
00:14:33,391 --> 00:14:34,531
This place has it all.

287
00:14:35,111 --> 00:14:36,271
Take my word for it,

288
00:14:36,291 --> 00:14:38,571
this is the perfect place to take that special someone

289
00:14:38,571 --> 00:14:39,691
on a romantic stroll.

290
00:14:40,551 --> 00:14:43,471
But why stop at one when I have two lovely ladies?

291
00:14:43,471 --> 00:14:48,731
This one is Nami, and this one here is Robin.

292
00:14:49,651 --> 00:14:51,311
You're scaring away the customers.

293
00:14:51,791 --> 00:14:53,251
Stop making that creepy face

294
00:14:53,251 --> 00:14:55,531
and tell me which water-water cabbage you want already.

295
00:14:55,931 --> 00:14:57,171
I'll take both of them.

296
00:14:57,231 --> 00:14:59,031
Oh, and there was something I wanted to ask you.

297
00:14:59,111 --> 00:15:00,651
I'm looking for some local specialties

298
00:15:00,651 --> 00:15:01,871
that are in season right now.

299
00:15:02,171 --> 00:15:03,111
So what have you got?

300
00:15:03,391 --> 00:15:05,731
Water-water apples and water-water pears.

301
00:15:06,331 --> 00:15:08,571
I can tell you where to find some fish too, if you'd like.

302
00:15:08,711 --> 00:15:10,531
My hubby runs a stand just like this one

303
00:15:10,531 --> 00:15:11,671
down at the seafood market.

304
00:15:11,951 --> 00:15:12,671
And where's that?

305
00:15:12,671 --> 00:15:14,851
First, go over there and get back on land.

306
00:15:15,051 --> 00:15:17,491
Take six rights, and then 17 lefts.

307
00:15:17,591 --> 00:15:18,771
You'll see it straight ahead.

308
00:15:19,671 --> 00:15:20,011
Huh?

309
00:15:21,271 --> 00:15:21,991
Let's see.

310
00:15:22,171 --> 00:15:23,471
Uh, this way?

311
00:15:24,871 --> 00:15:27,371
This place sure is a pain when you have to walk.

312
00:15:29,571 --> 00:15:32,111
If I don't stop running into dead ends

313
00:15:32,111 --> 00:15:33,951
and start figuring out where the hell I am,

314
00:15:34,011 --> 00:15:35,051
this food's gonna go bad

315
00:15:35,051 --> 00:15:36,491
before I make it back to the Mary.

316
00:15:36,771 --> 00:15:38,591
Don't tell me Zoro's brain fungus

317
00:15:38,591 --> 00:15:40,071
is starting to spread to me too.

318
00:15:42,671 --> 00:15:50,231
Robin, boy, am I glad to see you!

319
00:15:50,231 --> 00:15:51,631
It's me, Sanji!

320
00:15:51,771 --> 00:15:52,271
Yoo-hoo!

321
00:15:53,271 --> 00:15:55,371
Robin, look!

322
00:15:56,851 --> 00:15:57,351
Huh?

323
00:15:57,351 --> 00:16:00,731
She disappeared.

324
00:16:01,391 --> 00:16:03,011
Looks like another dead end to me.

325
00:16:03,751 --> 00:16:04,031
Huh.

326
00:16:04,471 --> 00:16:07,071
The only way to go is up, but there's...

327
00:16:07,071 --> 00:16:07,191
Huh?

328
00:16:11,031 --> 00:16:11,411
Usopp?

329
00:16:12,411 --> 00:16:14,451
Man, I don't know what's up with this place.

330
00:16:15,691 --> 00:16:17,571
I'm not going crazy, am I?

331
00:16:17,571 --> 00:16:32,451
Well, looky here.

332
00:16:32,731 --> 00:16:33,731
That must be the one.

333
00:16:37,811 --> 00:16:38,671
Pardon me.

334
00:16:38,671 --> 00:16:48,431
Well, it's just Usopp.

335
00:16:49,431 --> 00:16:50,231
My goodness.

336
00:16:50,571 --> 00:16:52,331
I know they told me their ship needed repairs,

337
00:16:52,491 --> 00:16:54,051
but I didn't think it would be in shambles.

338
00:16:57,491 --> 00:16:59,851
We should start by replacing this mast.

339
00:17:02,451 --> 00:17:02,851
Wait.

340
00:17:03,771 --> 00:17:04,731
That's not right.

341
00:17:05,231 --> 00:17:05,611
Hey!

342
00:17:05,811 --> 00:17:06,571
I see you!

343
00:17:06,751 --> 00:17:07,451
Don't move!

344
00:17:07,551 --> 00:17:08,111
You hear me?

345
00:17:08,111 --> 00:17:09,291
Oh, pardon.

346
00:17:09,591 --> 00:17:11,151
I didn't intend to disturb your nap.

347
00:17:11,691 --> 00:17:12,211
Jeez.

348
00:17:12,411 --> 00:17:13,671
That was a big surprise.

349
00:17:13,951 --> 00:17:16,431
I've never seen someone jump that far before.

350
00:17:16,951 --> 00:17:20,291
Well, my craftsmen are the best in the business for a reason.

351
00:17:26,091 --> 00:17:27,411
Who is this guy?

352
00:17:27,531 --> 00:17:28,291
Don't ask me.

353
00:17:28,771 --> 00:17:31,591
Extraordinary ships are not born from ordinary circumstances.

354
00:17:31,971 --> 00:17:34,431
They require a nearly superhuman level of skill.

355
00:17:35,151 --> 00:17:36,651
By the way, Kalifa...

356
00:17:36,651 --> 00:17:38,271
I've already finished the background check.

357
00:17:38,611 --> 00:17:40,391
The one in the middle is Straw Hat Luffy.

358
00:17:40,631 --> 00:17:44,031
His crew also includes Pirate Hunter Zorro and Nico Robin.

359
00:17:44,971 --> 00:17:47,491
Their combined bounty is 239 million.

360
00:17:47,771 --> 00:17:50,151
They have four other members without posted bounties,

361
00:17:50,251 --> 00:17:51,971
and their crew was formed in the East Blue.

362
00:17:52,971 --> 00:17:53,971
Named after their captain,

363
00:17:53,971 --> 00:17:56,351
they're known simply as the Straw Hat Pirates.

364
00:17:56,491 --> 00:17:57,831
They've really got our number.

365
00:17:58,291 --> 00:17:58,811
I see.

366
00:17:59,371 --> 00:17:59,691
Welcome.

367
00:18:00,131 --> 00:18:01,311
My name is Iceberg.

368
00:18:01,491 --> 00:18:02,471
I'm the boss here.

369
00:18:02,971 --> 00:18:05,171
And this is a mouse I just picked up.

370
00:18:05,731 --> 00:18:07,031
Its name is...

371
00:18:07,031 --> 00:18:07,691
Let's see.

372
00:18:08,691 --> 00:18:09,131
Tyrannosaurus.

373
00:18:09,131 --> 00:18:11,971
I'll need some rodent food in a cage.

374
00:18:12,231 --> 00:18:13,851
I've already taken care of that, sir.

375
00:18:14,311 --> 00:18:16,011
Well, amazing work, Kalifa.

376
00:18:16,191 --> 00:18:17,331
Just doing my job.

377
00:18:18,111 --> 00:18:19,091
Speaking of which,

378
00:18:19,251 --> 00:18:21,291
you're due at a hotel in Cheezer for lunch

379
00:18:21,291 --> 00:18:22,951
with a glassware factory executive.

380
00:18:23,331 --> 00:18:25,791
After that, you're set to make a speech at Liguria Square.

381
00:18:26,051 --> 00:18:27,191
And once that's finished,

382
00:18:27,351 --> 00:18:29,731
you'll be meeting with Mr. Bimini, the mayor of Pucci.

383
00:18:30,071 --> 00:18:32,631
Their local newspaper is scheduled to interview you as well.

384
00:18:32,631 --> 00:18:35,011
Oh, and when you return to the office,

385
00:18:35,171 --> 00:18:36,851
there's a folder full of important documents

386
00:18:36,851 --> 00:18:38,091
I need your signature on.

387
00:18:38,771 --> 00:18:39,631
I don't wanna.

388
00:18:41,051 --> 00:18:41,751
Very well.

389
00:18:41,851 --> 00:18:43,331
Then I'll cancel everything for you.

390
00:18:43,751 --> 00:18:44,211
Hold on!

391
00:18:44,251 --> 00:18:45,531
You're not serious, are you?

392
00:18:45,751 --> 00:18:47,071
What's the point of having power

393
00:18:47,071 --> 00:18:48,471
if I don't get to do what I want?

394
00:18:49,031 --> 00:18:50,171
If that's how you run things,

395
00:18:50,211 --> 00:18:51,911
you've got to be the worst mayor in the world.

396
00:18:52,111 --> 00:18:53,511
Yeah, Pops, that's pretty lazy.

397
00:18:53,891 --> 00:18:54,751
So, hold on.

398
00:18:54,891 --> 00:18:55,871
Are you sure this is the guy

399
00:18:55,871 --> 00:18:57,251
that old lady was talking about?

400
00:18:57,451 --> 00:18:57,771
Uh-huh.

401
00:18:58,131 --> 00:18:59,131
He's the guy, all right.

402
00:18:59,431 --> 00:19:00,631
Don't dare you!

403
00:19:02,631 --> 00:19:09,351
What's the big idea, lady?

404
00:19:09,571 --> 00:19:10,491
That was scary.

405
00:19:16,811 --> 00:19:18,231
I won't stand here

406
00:19:18,231 --> 00:19:19,451
and let you insolent louts

407
00:19:19,451 --> 00:19:22,071
disrespect one of the world's leading ship engineers.

408
00:19:22,231 --> 00:19:23,551
Hey, what's going on over there?

409
00:19:23,651 --> 00:19:26,231
Iceberg's secretary just flipped out on some pirates again.

410
00:19:26,271 --> 00:19:27,531
I wish she'd flip out on me.

411
00:19:27,931 --> 00:19:28,471
Pardon me.

412
00:19:28,711 --> 00:19:30,631
I get a bit carried away from time to time.

413
00:19:30,631 --> 00:19:33,831
Still, Iceberg is a respected figure here in Water 7,

414
00:19:33,971 --> 00:19:35,271
so try to show some courtesy.

415
00:19:35,871 --> 00:19:37,751
Yes, please don't provoke Khalifa.

416
00:19:38,291 --> 00:19:39,691
When she lashes out in anger,

417
00:19:39,951 --> 00:19:41,791
her rain sucks.

418
00:19:43,271 --> 00:19:44,691
We might have hurt his feelings,

419
00:19:44,771 --> 00:19:45,811
but you hurt his face!

420
00:19:45,851 --> 00:19:47,311
You're way meaner than us!

421
00:19:47,631 --> 00:19:49,071
Putting all that behind us,

422
00:19:49,091 --> 00:19:49,831
if you're Iceberg,

423
00:19:49,911 --> 00:19:51,111
you're the man we're looking for.

424
00:19:51,351 --> 00:19:53,071
Here, we brought a letter of introduction

425
00:19:53,071 --> 00:19:54,051
for you to look at.

426
00:19:54,231 --> 00:19:55,291
From Granny Kokoro?

427
00:19:55,291 --> 00:19:55,431
Oh?

428
00:19:56,531 --> 00:19:58,471
Take a look at their ship, it says.

429
00:19:58,471 --> 00:20:00,471
What?

430
00:20:01,491 --> 00:20:02,271
Are you serious?

431
00:20:02,631 --> 00:20:04,991
There has to be something we can do to change your mind.

432
00:20:05,131 --> 00:20:06,811
If money's the issue, we can pay.

433
00:20:06,951 --> 00:20:07,931
If you're really the best,

434
00:20:07,991 --> 00:20:09,771
then you've got to be the one to fix our Mary.

435
00:20:10,131 --> 00:20:11,111
Come on, we're begging.

436
00:20:11,371 --> 00:20:11,711
Okay.

437
00:20:11,951 --> 00:20:12,671
That's it?

438
00:20:12,891 --> 00:20:14,091
You don't need to think about it?

439
00:20:14,131 --> 00:20:14,331
Nope.

440
00:20:14,991 --> 00:20:16,071
Then why did you tear up the letter?

441
00:20:16,311 --> 00:20:17,971
The lipstick mark was creeping me out.

442
00:20:18,131 --> 00:20:21,151
Even so, Granny Kokoro and I have been drinking buddies for years.

443
00:20:21,311 --> 00:20:21,831
Well...

444
00:20:21,831 --> 00:20:24,111
And since Kako has already gone to make an assessment for me,

445
00:20:24,111 --> 00:20:26,111
it would seem like a waste to turn you down now.

446
00:20:26,251 --> 00:20:26,951
Wouldn't you say?

447
00:20:26,951 --> 00:20:31,191
Besides, it's not like I had anything better to do.

448
00:20:31,351 --> 00:20:32,611
Let me show you around the factory.

449
00:20:32,811 --> 00:20:33,271
Sound good?

450
00:20:33,371 --> 00:20:33,731
Sweet!

451
00:20:33,931 --> 00:20:34,771
Bring it on!

452
00:20:34,911 --> 00:20:35,911
Come along with me, then.

453
00:20:36,051 --> 00:20:38,131
Did he seriously cancel all of his meetings

454
00:20:38,131 --> 00:20:39,251
just to give us a tour?

455
00:20:39,371 --> 00:20:39,391
Yeah!

456
00:20:39,451 --> 00:20:41,671
You've got to let me sit on all the cool ships, okay?

457
00:20:42,471 --> 00:20:43,671
Oh, right.

458
00:20:43,791 --> 00:20:44,191
The money.

459
00:20:45,091 --> 00:20:45,311
Wait.

460
00:20:45,771 --> 00:20:46,051
What?

461
00:20:46,591 --> 00:20:46,811
No.

462
00:20:47,191 --> 00:20:48,291
No, no, no, no, no, no!

463
00:20:48,671 --> 00:20:48,971
No!

464
00:20:48,971 --> 00:20:49,471
No!

465
00:20:49,471 --> 00:20:49,671
No!

466
00:20:49,671 --> 00:20:49,751
No!

467
00:20:49,751 --> 00:20:49,831
No!

468
00:20:52,371 --> 00:20:52,811
Hey.

469
00:20:53,431 --> 00:20:54,391
What's wrong, Usopp?

470
00:20:54,451 --> 00:20:54,931
Come on.

471
00:20:54,931 --> 00:20:57,371
The money.

472
00:20:57,651 --> 00:20:58,851
It disappeared.

473
00:21:00,231 --> 00:21:01,731
Did he drop it somewhere?

474
00:21:02,071 --> 00:21:03,331
I don't know.

475
00:21:07,271 --> 00:21:07,911
Hey!

476
00:21:08,131 --> 00:21:09,131
Over there!

477
00:21:16,391 --> 00:21:16,991
Wait!

478
00:21:17,351 --> 00:21:18,251
Who are you?

479
00:21:18,371 --> 00:21:19,711
Why are you taking our money?

480
00:21:19,711 --> 00:21:21,491
I mean, give it back!

481
00:21:21,671 --> 00:21:23,031
We need it, you jerks!

482
00:21:23,251 --> 00:21:24,091
Do you hear me?

483
00:21:24,451 --> 00:21:24,831
Hey!

484
00:21:25,651 --> 00:21:28,391
Thanks for the cash.

485
00:21:29,011 --> 00:21:31,171
Give us our money back!

486
00:21:31,171 --> 00:21:32,451
All right.

487
00:21:32,451 --> 00:22:02,431
We'll be right back.

488
00:23:06,218 --> 00:23:11,018
But on the next One Piece, Gally Law Company, a grand site,

489
00:23:11,018 --> 00:23:15,018
Doc Number One, I'm going to be king of the pirates!

